<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/expert/solid-state-physics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Solid-state physics explains matter you can hold: why copper conducts, silicon semi-conducts,
    diamond doesn't, and magnets magnetise. The key idea is <strong>band structure</strong>: in a
    crystal, individual atomic energy levels smear into bands, and everything follows from
    whether the highest occupied band is full, empty, or half-full.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="opposite temperature responses — the crossing is why the same crystal can be tuned either way" widget="plotter" config={bandCfg} />

  <p>
    Metals conduct <em>worse</em> when heated (lattice vibrations scatter electrons); semiconductors conduct <em>better</em> (heat promotes electrons across the band gap). The crossing in the normalised plot marks why doping and temperature control are the whole art of semiconductor engineering.
  </p>

  <h2>Band structure, in three pictures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conductor:</strong> the top band is half-full — electrons drift at the slightest field. Copper's single 4s electron is the free-rider that wires your house.</li>
    <li><strong>Insulator:</strong> a wide gap (~5+ eV in diamond) separates the filled band from the empty one. No available states to move into, so no current, however gently pushed.</li>
    <li><strong>Semiconductor:</strong> a narrow gap (~1.1 eV in silicon) — thermal energy occasionally promotes an electron, leaving a mobile "hole". Add impurity atoms (doping) and you choose which carriers dominate: n-type or p-type.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why this subject runs the world</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The transistor (1947, Bell Labs):</strong> two junctions, one switch with no moving parts. Band engineering at the p–n junction is the entire computer industry's foundation — billions of them per chip.</li>
    <li><strong>Superconductivity (1911, explained 1957):</strong> below a critical temperature, resistance drops to exactly zero and magnetic fields are expelled (Meissner effect). BCS theory pairs electrons into bosonic Cooper pairs; the high-temperature variants discovered in 1986 remain partially unexplained.</li>
    <li><strong>Magnetism is quantum bookkeeping:</strong> unpaired electron spins aligning (ferromagnets) vs cancelling (antiferromagnets) — domains, hysteresis and hard drives are solid-state effects.</li>
    <li><strong>LEDs and lasers:</strong> electrons dropping across engineered band gaps emit photons of chosen colour — band structure by design, not discovery.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kittel, Introduction to Solid State Physics; Ashcroft & Mermin; Bardeen, Brattain & Shockley (1947).</p>
</TopicPage>
